course: dictionary
lesson: 5652
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. Liquidate is a good example. «The company was liquidated after it could not pay its debts.» The clues are company and debts, so liquidate means closed down, with everything sold to pay what it owed.
explain: Liquidate can also mean turning things you own into money. «She had to liquidate her shares to pay for the house.» The clue is the money. Ask yourself: is this about a business closing, selling things for cash, or something much darker?
explain: Liquidate is a formal word, so you will mostly meet it in business news and serious writing. When the clues are not clear, check a dictionary, and test your meaning by swapping in a simpler word such as sell or close down.
fact: Liquidate is a verb: liquidate, liquidates, liquidated, liquidating.
fact: To liquidate a company means to close it and sell what it owns to pay its debts.
fact: «Liquidate assets» means to sell the things you own to turn them into money.
fact: In stories and history, liquidate can mean to kill or get rid of enemies. This is a euphemism, a softer-sounding word for something terrible.
fact: Dictators (rulers with total power who cannot be voted out) have used the word this way, so when you meet it in a story, read carefully and think about who is doing what.
exercise: «The company was liquidated after it could not pay its debts.» What does liquidated mean here? | *Closed down, with everything sold to pay what it owed | Moved to a new town | Painted bright colours for a new start | Given away to the workers for free
exercise: «She had to liquidate her shares to pay for the house.» What does liquidate mean here? | *To turn things she owned into money | To give her shares to a friend | To buy more shares | To hide her money
exercise: Which clue in «The firm had huge debts and was liquidated» tells you it was a business closing? | The word huge | The word had | The word and | *The word debts
test: Nadia says you can swap in a simpler word to test your meaning. In «The company was liquidated», which word fits best? | Painted | *Closed down | Sang about | Visited
test: A story uses a word that sounds cold and formal, like liquidate, and you are not sure what it means. What should you do? | Guess that it means happy | Stop reading the story forever | Skip it and never think about it again | *Look at the clues around it, then check a dictionary
exam: «The dictator ordered his enemies to be liquidated.» What does liquidated mean in this sentence? | Politely asked to leave | Given a prize for their work | Taken on a holiday | *Killed, in a cold and formal way
